{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLAPt95SHHZZmqowuACK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I routed issue #215 to the engineer for both planning and implementation, and wrote `.ard/215/triage-lead.md` with status `done`, next `[triage:engineer]` and worker `engineer`.\n\nThe issue asks for a clear, scoped change, so it doesn't need a person to decide first. It adds a `route:` setting for each scanner, which controls whether its issues wait for a human or get triaged by an agent. It also adds a check that fails the whole run, filing nothing, if a finding looks like it contains a secret. It doesn't touch any of the files agents aren't allowed to change.\n\nThe file also tells maintainers three things:\n- **Missing roles:** this repository only has the engineer triage role. A new config key and a change to the shared scanner code would normally also get architect and documentation-planner input, so the engineer's plan has to cover those.\n- **Optional dependency:** the secret check can also use gitleaks when #89's setting is turned on. The plan should confirm the check works whether or not that setting exists.\n- **Size cap:** this issue is one part of a three-way split meant to stay under the worker's 1,000-line limit. If the plan comes out bigger than that, a maintainer may need to split it again.\n\nThere is only one reviewer (`eng-review`), and the config gives it no `when:` rule, so it runs on every pull request. That's why I added no `review:` entries."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":808,"cache_read_input_tokens":10784,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":808},"output_tokens":448,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess